<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Court</h1><p class="st-meta">1336 CE – 1392 CE · capital Yoshino</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rival imperial court founded by Emperor Go-Daigo in the mountains of Yoshino, which held out against the Ashikaga for more than half a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After his <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kenmu-restoration">Kenmu Restoration</a> collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a> escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> late in 1336 and established his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshino-nara">Yoshino</a> in the mountains south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>, insisting that the regalia he carried made him the only rightful emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Southern Court relied on loyal warrior families such as the Kusunoki, Kitabatake and Nitta, and on regional bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, where Prince Kaneyoshi held power for years and dealt with Ming envoys. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitabatake-chikafusa">Kitabatake Chikafusa</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinno-shotoki">Jinno shotoki</a> to argue its legitimacy. Its emperors after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-murakami">Go-Murakami</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-chokei">Chokei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-kameyama">Go-Kameyama</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Militarily outmatched, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-kameyama">Go-Kameyama</a> agreed in 1392 to hand the regalia to the Northern emperor on a promise that the lines would alternate, which was never kept. In 1911 the government declared the Southern line legitimate.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1336 CE – 1392 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Korea and Japan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.29%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1336 CE – 1392 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yoshino</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Go-Daigo, Go-Murakami, Go-Kameyama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shinto, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nancho, Yoshino Court, Southern Dynasty (Japan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=southern-court" aria-label="Southern Court on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8135 932 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Court lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8455 1188L8441 1168M8455 1188L8441 1167"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0M8441 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8455 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8455 1188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yoshino. <a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=southern-court">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
